---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const formula = (tex: string) => renderFormula(tex, false);

const labels = [
  { id: 'coefficient', tex: 'd^2/2', blue: false },
  { id: 'cost', tex: 'm^2/2', blue: false },
  { id: 'line', tex: String.raw`d=m\sqrt r`, blue: true },
  { id: 'strict', tex: String.raw`d>m\sqrt r`, blue: true },
  { id: 'nine', tex: String.raw`3=1\sqrt9`, blue: false },
  { id: 'old', tex: String.raw`\frac{117}{37}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'root', tex: String.raw`\sqrt{10}\approx3.1623`, blue: true },
  { id: 'remaining', tex: 'r-9', blue: false },
];
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-curve-budget"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  description="A film about curves through fixed points, the coefficients each point requires, and the degree threshold in Nagata's conjecture. It illustrates the approved proof with a pinched cubic and interpolation. A complete text transcript follows."
  caption="Curves through ten scattered points: why counting coefficients suggests the line d = m√r, why nine points sit on it and ten points must stay above it, and how the approved proof turns that into interpolation one row at a time."
  leftOut="Seshadri constants and the nef class; Hilbert's fourteenth problem; the earlier bounds and cone constructions; the cyclic product, normal polynomial and ruled surface; the interpolation matrix and row-count conditions; the common power of the curve; the SHGH conjecture; the Lean comparator. The proof drawings are schematic."
>
  <Fragment slot="labels">
    {labels.map((label) => <span class:list={['film-label', 'film-label--small', { 'film-label--blue': label.blue }]} data-label={label.id}><span set:html={formula(label.tex)} /></span>)}
    <span class="film-label film-label--stamp" data-label="status"><span>APPROVED · PLANE CURVES</span></span>
  </Fragment>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The conic, cubic and nodal cubic are solved at the ten displayed rational pins. Their real zero sets are traced numerically. The nodal cubic has both first derivatives zero at pin 8. A degree <span set:html={formula('d')} /> polynomial has <span set:html={formula('(d+1)(d+2)/2')} /> coefficients; multiplicity <span set:html={formula('m')} /> at one point imposes <span set:html={formula('m(m+1)/2')} /> linear conditions.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The eight plotted minimum degrees are certified at these pins by full column ranks modulo the prime 67,108,859 one degree below each dot. At the displayed degree there are more coefficients than conditions. The strict inequality <span set:html={formula(String.raw`d>m\sqrt r`)} /> is necessary, not sufficient for every integer pair. For example, <span set:html={formula(String.raw`m=6,\ d=19`)} /> lies above the line but has 210 independent conditions on 210 coefficients and no nonzero curve.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The theorem concerns very general points and every multiplicity. This finite calculation through <span set:html={formula('m=8')} /> illustrates it but does not prove it. The moving tenth pin, the pinched model cubic, the exponent triangle and the supposed curve below the line are schematic. Tevelev's notes support the plane-curve theorem; the three companion papers remain unreviewed in the article's dated evidence.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
